WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.920
John Larroquette is a familiar face&nbsp;
to fans of sitcoms and legal dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.480
and a well-known voice to horror-heads. He's&nbsp;
been plenty busy since "Night Court" first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.200
ended in 1992, but if you missed&nbsp;
his many turns, here's a rundown.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.160
More than thirty years after its&nbsp;
final episode aired, "Night Court"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.900
is back. The reaction has been mixed, but some&nbsp;
critics, like William Hughes at the A.V. Club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.000
insist that once the reboot hits its stride,&nbsp;
it's a worthwhile watch. Hughes rhetorically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.660
asks why a "Night Court" reboot needs to exist and&nbsp;
provides a two-fold answer. First, it provides a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.980
new sitcom home for "The Big Bang Theory" star&nbsp;
Melissa Rauch. For the second, Hughes writes,

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:45.240
The original "Night Court" imprint is all&nbsp;
over the new series. Rauch's Judge Abby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.380
Stone is the daughter of Judge Harry Stone,&nbsp;
who was played by the late Harry Anderson.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.893
"I moved here for a job. I am&nbsp;
the new night court judge."

00:00:53.893 --> 00:00:54.393
[scream]

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.160
Yet Larroquette is the only original cast&nbsp;
member to return as a regular in the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.660
series as prosecutor Dan Fielding. So what&nbsp;
led him back to Judge Stone's courtroom?

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.740
"Night Court" was by no means the&nbsp;
actor's first go-around in sitcoms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.540
His very first TV appearance was in an episode&nbsp;
of "Sanford and Son." He also appeared in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.560
classic sitcoms of the era, including&nbsp;
"Three's Company" and "Mork &amp; Mindy."

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.520
After he broke out with "Night Court," it's&nbsp;
not all that surprising that Larroquette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.020
jumped right into starring in his own sitcom,&nbsp;
"The John Larroquette Show." Premiering on NBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.100
in the fall of 1993, the show revolved&nbsp;
around Larroquette's John Hemingway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.200
the manager of a bus depot in St. Louis. Themes&nbsp;
usually involved recovering alcoholic Hemingway's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.180
sobriety and membership in Alcoholics Anonymous,&nbsp;
topics with which Larroquette was familiar.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.380
The series struggled to attract viewers.&nbsp;
Though its premise underwent some retooling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.680
after the end of its first season in&nbsp;
an attempt to lighten its atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.480
it was abruptly canceled only one&nbsp;
month into its fourth season in 1996.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.160
The following year, Larroquette made his&nbsp;
first appearance as Joey Heric on the ABC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.900
legal drama "The Practice." First appearing as one&nbsp;
of attorney Bobby Donnell's clients in Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.280
Joey is revealed to be narcissistic and&nbsp;
conniving, willing to manipulate the legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.440
system to his own ends and literally get&nbsp;
away with murder, not just once but twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.360
When he returns in Season 6, he passes&nbsp;
the bar and becomes an attorney himself.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:18.134
"Can't anybody say, 'Hey, good to see ya?'"

00:02:18.134 --> 00:02:22.800
Larroquette's sinister Heric ended up appearing&nbsp;
in six episodes of the David E. Kelley drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.800
"The Practice" ended in 2004, and spun off into a&nbsp;
new series that year, "Boston Legal." Larroquette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.300
joined the cast of that series in 2007, but&nbsp;
not as Heric, as a totally new character.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:38.580
He played Carl Sack, senior partner at the&nbsp;
firm Crane, Poole &amp; Schmidt. In Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.480
Sack transfers from New York to the&nbsp;
Boston office. He is initially put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.380
off by the Bostonians' more carefree&nbsp;
attitude, but eventually finds his home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.820
among them. Larroquette remained a regular&nbsp;
on the series for its last two seasons.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.460
Larroquette has made a habit&nbsp;
out of playing lawyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.440
so it's fitting he portrayed mystery-solving&nbsp;
former-LAPD-officer-turned-attorney Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.680
McBride in all ten entries in&nbsp;
the "McBride" TV movie series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.640
which aired on the Hallmark&nbsp;
Channel between 2005 and 2008.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.900
Larroquette himself directed four of the&nbsp;
10 movies, in which McBride investigated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.320
the murders of a therapist, a&nbsp;
radio host, a Hollywood madam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.620
and many others. All had punchy titles like&nbsp;
"McBride: Murder Past Midnight" or "McBride:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.720
Tune in for Murder." There was even an entry that&nbsp;
focused on a murder at a dog show called "McBride:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.260
Dogged." Larroquette's son Jonathan&nbsp;
provided music for some of the movies.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.860
A non-lawyer thread that has run&nbsp;
throughout Larroquette's career is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.360
one you may not know about, since his face&nbsp;
is never shown in any of the movies. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.140
has narrated numerous entries in the&nbsp;
"Texas Chainsaw Massacre" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:46.440
starting with the very first film in 1974.&nbsp;
That movie was his very first acting job.

00:03:46.757 --> 00:03:51.317
"For them, an idyllic summer&nbsp;
afternoon drive became a nightmare."

00:03:51.317 --> 00:03:55.320
Larroquette told Parade magazine that&nbsp;
he had known director Tobe Hooper at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.620
least five years before the movie was&nbsp;
made. Hooper called in a favor from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.400
his friend and was so strapped for cash&nbsp;
that he paid him with a bag of cannabis.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.060
Other films for which Larroquette&nbsp;
has provided narration include the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.880
2003 "Texas Chainsaw Massacre" remake, its&nbsp;
2006 prequel "The Texas Chainsaw Massacre:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.300
The Beginning," and 2022's&nbsp;
"Texas Chainsaw Massacre."

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.760
Despite his crucial contribution to the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.414
the actor insists that horror isn't really his&nbsp;
genre. He has never watched any of the films.

